ickle scanning", "url": "https://huggingface.co/docs/hub/security-pickle", "publisher": "Hugging Face documentation"}, {"title": "Data scientists targeted by malicious Hugging Face ML models with silent backdoor", "url": "https://jfrog.com/blog/data-scientists-targeted-by-malicious-hugging-face-ml-models-with-silent-backdoor/", "publisher": "JFrog"}, {"title": "Taming the wild west of ML: practical model signing with Sigstore", "url": "https://blog.sigstore.dev/model-transparency-v1.0", "publisher": "Sigstore blog", "date": "Apr 2025"}, {"title": "Taming the wild west of ML: practical model signing", "url": "https://blog.google/security/taming-wild-west-of-ml-practical-mode/", "publisher": "Google", "date": "2025"}, {"title": "SLSA security levels (v1.1)", "url": "https://slsa.dev/spec/v1.1/levels", "publisher": "SLSA"}, {"title": "Machine Learning Bill of Materials (ML-BOM)", "url": "https://cyclonedx.org/capabilities/mlbom/", "publisher": "CycloneDX"}, {"title": "SafePickle: robust and generic ML detection of malicious pickle-based ML models", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.19818", "publisher": "arXiv", "date": "Feb 2026"}]}-->
<p>Software teams have spent a decade learning to verify what they run: signed packages, pinned dependencies, software bills of materials, reproducible builds. AI teams often skip all of it. A model is downloaded from a public hub by name, loaded with a single line of code and deployed. If that file had been tampered with, or was malicious from the start, few organisations would notice.</p>
<p>As more companies run open-weight models privately, as in <a href="/insights/private-llm-on-prem/">private LLMs on-prem</a>, this becomes a real attack surface.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>The risks</h2>
<p><strong>Code execution on load.</strong> Hugging Face's own documentation warns that pickle, the default format for PyTorch weights, can enable arbitrary code execution when a file is loaded, and recommends safetensors as a safer alternative<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Security researchers have reported malicious models on public hubs that used this to plant backdoors, and noted that pickle isn't the only risky format: some Keras models can run code through Lambda layers<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>Scanners are imperfect.</strong> A 2026 study of malicious pickle-based models reported that existing scanners varied widely in how well they detected them on its dataset, which is a reason not to treat a scan as proof of safety<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>Tampering and substitution.</strong> Without signatures, there's no reliable way to know a model file is the one its publisher released, or that the copy in your registry hasn't changed since it was approved.</p>
<p><strong>Unknown lineage.</strong> Fine-tuned models inherit everything about their base model and training data, including licence terms and possible poisoning. Without records, you can't answer basic questions in a security review or <a href="/insights/ai-startup-technical-due-diligence-checklist/">due diligence</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The building blocks</h2>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it gives you</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Safe formats</td><td>Weights that load as data rather than executable code, such as safetensors<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>Model signing</td><td>Verification that a model is unchanged and from the expected publisher. The OpenSSF model-signing project released v1.0 in April 2025, built on Sigstore and supporting any model format and size<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>Build provenance</td><td>A record of how an artefact was produced. SLSA's build track defines levels from provenance existing (L1), to signed provenance from a hosted build platform (L2), to a hardened build platform (L3)<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup></td></tr>
<tr><td>ML bill of materials</td><td>An inventory of the model, datasets and dependencies. CycloneDX supports ML-BOMs and model cards for transparency into security, privacy, safety and ethical considerations<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<div class="callout"><p>Treat a model like a container image: pulled from a trusted registry, pinned by digest, verified on deployment, and traceable to how it was built.</p></div>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>The checklist</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Use an internal model registry.</strong> Production never pulls directly from public hubs. Models are imported, reviewed and stored internally.</li>
<li><strong>Prefer safe formats.</strong> Accept safetensors or equivalent where possible; convert or reject formats that execute code on load. Load untrusted files only in isolated sandboxes.</li>
<li><strong>Scan on import</strong>, while remembering scanners miss things. Scanning reduces risk; it doesn't certify safety.</li>
<li><strong>Pin by hash, not by name.</strong> Reference models by immutable digest so "latest" can never silently change what runs.</li>
<li><strong>Sign what you produce and verify what you run.</strong> Sign fine-tuned and approved models at the end of your pipeline, and verify signatures at deployment.</li>
<li><strong>Generate provenance in your training pipeline</strong>, aiming for at least signed provenance from a managed build platform, in the spirit of SLSA Build L2.</li>
<li><strong>Keep an ML-BOM per model</strong>: base model, datasets, licences, training configuration and evaluation results, linked to your AI system register.</li>
<li><strong>Restrict runtime permissions.</strong> Model-serving containers need no outbound internet access and minimal privileges, enforced with the policies in <a href="/insights/kubernetes-admission-policies-cel/">Kubernetes admission policies</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Re-evaluate after every model change</strong> with your <a href="/insights/llm-evaluation-sets-that-predict-production/">evaluation set</a>, including safety and behaviour tests, not just accuracy.</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Beyond the weights</h2>
<p>The supply chain extends past model files. Datasets, tokenisers, prompt templates, agent tools and MCP servers all shape behaviour. MCP servers in particular deserve the same allow-listing and review, as covered in <a href="/insights/securing-mcp-servers-checklist/">securing MCP servers</a>. Google's announcement of model signing noted plans to extend the approach to datasets and other ML artefacts<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>, which is the right direction.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Why this matters now</h2>
<p>Enterprise customers and regulators are starting to ask where models come from and how they are controlled. An ML-BOM, signed models and a registry answer those questions concretely, and support governance frameworks such as those compared in <a href="/insights/iso-42001-vs-nist-ai-rmf/">ISO 42001 and the NIST AI RMF</a>. They are also inexpensive to adopt early and painful to retrofit after an incident.</p>
</section>

